I also consider it one of the first object types to bless from the
list of objects you mentioned here (tough mostly because the others
are still less necessary to bless :-).  But as long as potions are
limited in the early game and you don't need it; and want to prefer
using your holy water for other applications, like increasing AC or
weapons anyway at that stage.

> [blessing a luckstone]
> It's more convenient that way, and safer once you get far enough to
> meet monsters that can curse your inventory. 

Even then; you should inspect the curse status anyway after you had
an encounter with spellcasters. No need to use it in advance on an
object that might not even get cursed, and then it's missing for a
really necessary object.

It's also not bad if your luckstone gets cursed for just a couple
turns. Often you also have some spare luckstones in your bag and
just drop the cursed one, thus saving holy water.

> It doesn't really make
> all _that_ much difference, but I like to bless mine anyway.

With every "invested" holy water you reduce your flexibility. Why
spend a holy water that might be missing later, to bless a scroll
of remove curse for example. I don't think it's a good suggestion
to bless just "for convenience".

> [bless magic lamps]
> I do like to keep a magic lamp as a light source, but I still bless
> it, just in case I should ever decide I want the wish after all.

It seems you are generally talking from the perspective that you have
as much holy water as you like with you; and while it's true that one
indeed can dilute as many potions as one likes to have quite infinite
amounts of holy water,[*] in practise you should delay in applying it
because you're taking away flexibility and removing options that you
otherwise have.

[*] And you might even decide to delay diluting *every* potion that
might become useful later.

The only point where I usually apply holy water in advance is when I
have enough of it and could reduce my burdeness status by applying it
(in cases where I don't want or can't put the holy water in my stash).

